# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values. .env is gitignored.
#
# STEP 1 — authentication only. Sessions are a signed cookie, so there is no
# database yet. DATABASE_URL / DIRECT_URL for the local Postgres container
# (docker-compose, same setup as PbN Proposals) arrive with the schema in Step 2.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public origin. Used to build the Google redirect URI and every sign-in
# redirect, and later the shareable prospect link.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ means this is INLINED AT BUILD TIME, not read at runtime. In
# production it must be the real deployed origin, and changing it later requires
# a REDEPLOY — editing the variable alone does nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ===========================================================================
# AUTH
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET and SESSION_SECRET are REQUIRED — sign
# in is live, and (from Step 2) the public prospect page is the only thing in
# front of it. Leave any of them unset and the sign-in screen says which one.
#
# Three levels, narrowing: signed in (@practicenumbers.com), SDR_ACCESS_EMAILS
# (create, edit and publish your own prospects), ADMIN_EMAILS (everything, plus
# All Prospects and Analytics in Steps 4–5).
# ===========================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google OAuth — the sign-in provider, and the only one. Employees sign in with
# their @practicenumbers.com Workspace account; there are no passwords.
#
# Create at: Google Cloud console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials ->
# Create credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application.
#
#   1. On the CONSENT SCREEN, choose user type "Internal", not "External".
#      Internal makes Google itself refuse accounts outside the Workspace org,
#      so the domain restriction comes from the platform and not only from our
#      code. It is only offered if the GCP project belongs to the
#      practicenumbers.com organization.
#
#   2. The AUTHORIZED REDIRECT URI must be registered per environment and
#      matched EXACTLY — a trailing slash or http-vs-https mismatch is a hard
#      `redirect_uri_mismatch`. It is always this origin plus the callback path:
#
#        http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback
#        https://<your-deployed-origin>/api/auth/google/callback
#
#      i.e. NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL + GOOGLE_CALLBACK_PATH from
#      src/server/auth/config.ts. Note the segment order: google/callback.
#
# The PbN Proposals OAuth client already registers the localhost URI above, so
# for local work its credentials can be reused as-is.
#
# Scopes are `openid email profile` and nothing else. Authorized JavaScript
# origins can stay EMPTY — the code exchange happens on the server.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID is deliberately NOT prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_. The redirect to
# Google is built server-side. The secret must never be prefixed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="000000000000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com"
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="GOCSPX-..."

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing key for the session cookie. Generate a fresh one per environment:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Rotating this signs everyone out, which is the intended way to force a
# re-login. Do NOT reuse the local value in production, and never commit one.
#
# The permitted email domain is a CONSTANT IN CODE, not an env var: it does not
# vary between environments, and an env var that was accidentally empty in
# production would silently disable the domain check.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SESSION_SECRET="generate-me-with-openssl-rand-base64-32"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who gets admin. Comma-separated emails, matched case-insensitively against
# the address Google returns.
#
# An env allowlist rather than a `role` column, so there is no schema and no
# promote/demote UI. Read at request time: adding someone locally takes a
# dev-server restart, in production a redeploy.
#
# EMPTY OR UNSET MEANS NOBODY IS ADMIN. That is the deliberate direction to
# fail — "unset means everyone" would turn a blank variable into an open door.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_EMAILS="nikhil.kumar@practicenumbers.com"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who may CREATE, EDIT and PUBLISH a prospect — the SDR team.
#
# Signing in is the earlier gate and covers the whole company; this decides who
# may write. Same rules as ADMIN_EMAILS: comma-separated, case-insensitive,
# read at request time, and EMPTY MEANS NOBODY rather than everybody. Admins are
# always included.
#
# Enforced in the Server Action, not only in the page. Hiding a form does not
# stop a POST at the endpoint behind it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SDR_ACCESS_EMAILS="first.sdr@practicenumbers.com,second.sdr@practicenumbers.com"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HubSpot — optional. Unset, the app still works end to end: the dialog after
# publishing is skipped and the tracking popup opens straight away.
#
# A PRIVATE APP token, not OAuth and not a legacy API key. There is one HubSpot
# portal — ours — so there is nothing for anyone to "connect":
#
#   Settings -> Integrations -> Private Apps -> Create a private app
#   Scopes: crm.objects.contacts.write (crm.objects.contacts.read is not needed)
#
# The value starts with "pat-". The same portal as PbN Proposals, so the same
# token works for both tools.
#
# Server-side only. It can rewrite any contact in the CRM, so it must never be
# prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_; it is read only inside a Server Action.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HUBSPOT_ACCESS_TOKEN="pat-na1-..."

# The INTERNAL name of the contact property the prospect link is written to —
# the value shown under Settings -> Properties, not its display label. Create it
# once, as a single-line text or URL property on Contacts, labelled "Prospect
# Link". A DIFFERENT property from Proposals' "Proposal Link", so the two tools
# never overwrite each other on the same contact.
#
# Optional. Defaults to sdr_prospect_link, the property created in our portal
# for this tool — verify it matches before relying on the default, since a
# mismatch is reported as a 400 rather than silently ignored.
HUBSPOT_PROSPECT_LINK_PROPERTY="sdr_prospect_link"
